<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Augusto Pinochet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1915">1915</a>–<a href="/years/2006">2006</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Augusto Pinochet was a Chilean general who led the military coup of 11 September 1973 that overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador-allende">Salvador Allende</a>. He ruled Chile as dictator until 1990, with widespread repression and free-market economic reforms, and imposed the constitution of 1980. After losing the plebiscite of 1988 he handed power to an elected president.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augusto-pinochet.jpg" alt="Augusto Pinochet" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ministerio de Relaciones Exteriores de Chile · CC BY 2.0 cl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augusto_Pinochet_foto_oficial(b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Salvador Allende, elected in 1970 at the head of the Popular Unity coalition, was overthrown on 11 September 1973 by the armed forces under <b>Augusto Pinochet</b>, whose regime killed or caused the disappearance of more than 3,000 people and imprisoned and tortured tens of thousands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Officers seized power in Egypt in 1952, Brazil in 1964, Chile in 1973, when General <b>Augusto Pinochet</b> overthrew the elected government of Salvador Allende, and Argentina in 1976, and repeatedly in Nigeria, Ghana and many other African states; independence leaders banned rival parties; and personal rulers such as…</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>One member usually presides and often becomes head of state, and a strong president can turn a junta into a personal dictatorship, as <b>Augusto Pinochet</b> largely did in Chile.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Augusto Pinochet</b>’s rule in Chile used authoritarian means to secure investment, bringing growth and high inequality, and Suharto’s extirpation of the Communist Party of Indonesia allowed capitalism to expand there.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>In Latin America most nations were under military rule by the mid-1970s; <b>Augusto Pinochet</b> ruled Chile from 1973 to 1990.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>…1973 on the live album Primer festival internacional de la canción popular (DICAP label), which Quilapayún closed with it, and was recorded in Chile at a large Quilapayún concert in the Alameda in Santiago, three months before <b>Augusto Pinochet</b>'s coup d'état overthrew Allende and began the military dictatorship.</blockquote><a href="/songs/el-pueblo-unido-jamas-sera-vencido">El pueblo unido jamás será vencido</a></li></ul></section>
